WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.260
Narrator: The Pokémon
in "Detective Pikachu"

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:05.570
have a new look, and they've
never seemed more real

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:09.350
or terrifying. That's in
large part thanks to this guy.

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:12.970
RJ Palmer: I'm RJ Palmer, I'm
a concept artist at Ubisoft,

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:14.360
I draw creatures and monsters,

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.480
and I worked on "Detective Pikachu."

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:17.313
Narrator: He started drawing

00:00:17.313 --> 00:00:19.180
when he was just three years old.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:20.550
He continued to hone his craft

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.670
at the San Francisco Academy of Art.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:26.310
Then in 2012, his drawings
for an amateur fan art project

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:28.640
gained so much attention on the internet,

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.860
it ultimately landed him a
job on the new Pokémon film.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.820
RJ: So I started drawing a shark dinosaur,

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.350
and that turned into the Pokémon Garchomp,

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.700
and people really, really responded to it,

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.860
so I kept drawing more.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.500
Narrator: RJ used real
animals as inspiration.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.020
RJ: The natural world is so inspiring,

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:46.780
and I think pulling from that,

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.270
as much as possible, is really cool.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:50.840
Narrator: For example, Mewtwo

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.813
was a combination of several creatures.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.060
RJ: Kangaroo, and a little bit of rhino,

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:57.980
and hairless cat, and some barn owl.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.590
Narrator: For Charizard, he
focused on different lizards.

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:02.510
RJ: So I looked at a
lot of monitor lizards

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.740
and brought qualities of crocodilians

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.020
in there that I liked, as well.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.080
Narrator: Surprisingly enough,

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.100
RJ's main tools for designing
his realistic Pokémon

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.100
were simply a Wacom tablet and Photoshop.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.300
RJ: And I've got a second monitor up,

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:15.740
so I can have all my references.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:17.470
Narrator: Ultimately, it
was the amount of time

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:19.410
and effort RJ poured into the project

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:21.500
that made his Pokémon come to life.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.860
RJ researched every animal
he used for inspiration,

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:26.970
and in the end, each Pokémon he created

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:28.800
took 30 or more hours to complete.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.080
RJ: The one that took the longest to make

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:31.930
is Tyrantrum.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:33.540
Tyrantrum is my favorite Pokémon.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:34.373
Narrator: He posted them on

00:01:34.373 --> 00:01:37.890
the popular fan art
community site, DeviantArt,

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.240
and his work won over
fans on the internet.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:41.430
It also got the attention

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:43.980
of the production designer
of "Detective Pikachu."

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:45.477
RJ: He gives me a call, and he's like,

00:01:45.477 --> 00:01:47.050
"Would you like to work
on the Pokémon movie?"

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:48.930
And I'm in shock, it was amazing.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:52.370
I've always been really into
Pokémon as just a franchise,

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:56.120
because I think it's
really fun and creative.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:02.863
Narrator: He moved to LA

00:02:02.863 --> 00:02:04.830
and worked on the film for seven months,

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.980
doing concept art for
characters and the environments.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:11.150
He helped come up with the
initial designs for the Pokémon.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:12.990
RJ: And then it gets given to

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:14.470
all sorts of crazy effects houses.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:16.100
Narrator: While his Pokémon on DeviantArt

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.700
are more realistic, the ones
he designed for the film

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.028
had to be a bit more traditional.

00:02:21.028 --> 00:02:24.050
RJ: So working on the movie,
they had to get more in line

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:25.990
with the cartoon aesthetic, I think.

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:28.900
You do a design, and the
people have opinions,

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:30.080
and then you do another design,

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:31.930
and they also still have opinions,

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.990
and so it's a constant back and forth,

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:36.920
trying to make one person happy,

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:38.840
which is going to make
somebody else unhappy,

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:41.100
and you have to try and
find a middle ground

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.680
that's going to work for people.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:45.650
Narrator: Pikachu, as the star
of the film and franchise,

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.093
took a while to nail down and get right.

00:02:47.093 --> 00:02:48.594
Pikachu: Pika pika!

00:02:48.594 --> 00:02:51.559
Woman: Yeah, pika pika
pika, he's adorable!

00:02:51.559 --> 00:02:53.100
RJ: Pikachu's one of the most

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.110
well known cartoon characters ever, right?

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.360
Narrator: Despite speaking english

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:59.100
and being a bit more
furry than the cartoon,

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.160
the Pikachu in the film

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.730
is different from his realistic design.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:04.280
RJ: I based him on rodents and lagomorphs.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:06.640
Lagomorphs are like rabbits,

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:09.510
just like small, woodland critters.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:10.810
Narrator: But it was Mr. Mime

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:13.260
that really grabbed the
internet's attention.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.680
Since his debut in the
trailer, he's become a meme.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.540
RJ: Mr. Mime was always
going to be weird, right?

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:19.373
'Cause what do you make that thing?

00:03:19.373 --> 00:03:20.670
When I did my realistic version of it,

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:24.460
I made it a frog, because it
can be so many weird things.

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:26.740
I think a detail that I didn't know

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:27.620
they were going to go for,

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:30.870
but the little shoulder pauldrons

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:32.562
actually look like kickballs.

00:03:32.562 --> 00:03:34.640
I thought that was a pretty fun idea.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.660
And the Jigglypuff I like.
In the Pokédex entry,

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:40.260
they talk about it having a
balloon-like skin texture,

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:43.410
which would probably be
pretty creepy in real life.

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:44.860
Narrator: So they made it more furry

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:47.500
with a curly wisp of
hair. In the end though,

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:49.710
it's the fans who have the final say.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:51.680
RJ: Everybody has their own
idea of what Pokémon is,

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.010
and I've certainly encountered
that working on my own stuff.

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:58.940
I've had some very divisive
takes on certain Pokémon.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:00.990
So I knew that was going to be a problem

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:05.060
going into the movie,
and so far, at least,

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:06.210
the response to the trailer,

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.750
at least from the circles that I've seen,

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:10.083
seem pretty positive.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:12.780
Pikachu: Hi, yes, okay, a can?

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:14.070
Shoving? Pushing?

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:15.210
My problem is that I push people away

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:16.440
and then hate them for leaving?

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:17.920
Tim: He's saying you can shove it.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.240
Pikachu: What, I can shove it?

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:20.650
Okay, that's it, no,
we're switching rolls.

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:21.483
I'm bad cop, you're good cop.

00:04:21.483 --> 00:04:23.280
Tim: No no no no no, we're not cops.

